Frame injection for qcacld-3.0 monitor mode.

Stock wlan_mon_drv_ops has no .ndo_start_xmit -- the upstream comment reads
"does not Tx and most of operations" -- so a radiotap frame written to the
monitor netdev is dropped by the network stack before the driver ever sees it.
This adds a Tx handler that strips the radiotap header and hands the 802.11
frame to wlan_cfg80211_mgmt_tx(), the same call cfg80211's .mgmt_tx uses to
reach the firmware.

The QCA firmware silently drops frames submitted on a monitor vdev
(WMI_VDEV_TYPE_MONITOR), so injection is performed through a hidden STA vdev
(WMI_VDEV_TYPE_STA) created when the monitor interface is brought up. A self
peer is created on the hidden STA vdev so the firmware accepts injected
frames. The hidden vdev is torn down when the monitor interface is stopped or
when the module is unloaded.

Why this can work at all: __hdd_mon_open() calls hdd_start_adapter() and sets
SME_SESSION_OPENED, so a monitor-mode adapter owns a real vdev. That makes
hdd_objmgr_get_vdev_by_user() succeed, which is the precondition monitor mode
would otherwise fail. Note that __wlan_hdd_mgmt_tx()'s own device_mode
branches are all skipped for QDF_MONITOR_MODE, which is why this calls
wlan_cfg80211_mgmt_tx() directly rather than reusing that wrapper.

Limitations, in order of how likely they are to bite:

  * The hidden STA vdev must be on the same channel as the intended transmit
    channel.  Because the injection path passes NULL for the channel, the
    hidden STA vdev's current channel is used.  Userspace is responsible for
    ensuring the monitor and hidden STA vdevs are tuned to the desired
    channel.
  * Radiotap is parsed only far enough to find where the 802.11 frame begins.
    Requested rate, retries and TX_NOACK are ignored; the firmware chooses.
  * Data and control frames are handed to the same entry point.
    wlan_cfg80211_mgmt_tx() is a management-frame API, so those are the most
    likely to be rejected.
  * The 20 dBm world-regulatory cap and the "no IR" flag on channels 12-14
    still apply; they are enforced by cfg80211 and the firmware, not here.
    See README.md#regulatory-database.

Frames are dropped, never requeued, on any error: returning anything other
than NETDEV_TX_OK from ndo_start_xmit would make the stack retry a frame that
can never succeed.

